description:
In handle_metadata_changes (src/metadata.rs:82-86 and :91-101), both filterRelatedType and filterMetadataType parse via:

    .and_then(|v| match v {
        Value::String(s) => Some(s),
        Value::Null => None,
        _ => None,
    });

The Value::Null arm is dead — the catch-all _ => None handles it identically. Idiomatic Rust collapses to:

    .and_then(|v| match v {
        Value::String(s) => Some(s),
        _ => None,
    });

What goes wrong as-is: a reader pauses on the explicit Null arm assuming Null has different meaning than 'any other shape' (e.g. that Null is expected and other shapes are errors). The comment-free side-by-side arms imply a distinction that does not exist. Clippy's match_same_arms lint flags this. Same idiom applies to the inner filter_metadata_type match at lines 94-97 (Value::String(s) => Some(s), _ => None) which is already collapsed correctly — so the outer match is inconsistent with the inner one in the same function.
